{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-88581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a class, $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{12}$ of them play basketball. The probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15}.$ If a girl is selected at random, what is the probability that she plays basketball?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen student plays basketball, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{12}$ of the boys play basketball, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(A|{B}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{12})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(A|{B}^{′})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{15}) & =60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}))​ \\\\ 4 & =3+24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 1 & =24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ P(A|{B}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected girl plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{24}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd8f22e9333d6669fcc7a6f46cf10d8d22a1463c2e65ac245db184e6b01e1bab"} {"question" "q-88581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{24}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{40}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{120}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{100}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $55%$ are produced by machine $B.$ If $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $55%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.08,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.55) \\\\  & =0.058.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd8f22e9333d6669fcc7a6f46cf10d8d22a1463c2e65ac245db184e6b01e1bab"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.035"] ["math" "0.058"] ["math" "0.085"] ["math" "0.110"] ["math" "0.120"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-88978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.058"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Robert has a desk with two drawers, drawer $A$ and drawer $B.$ Of the pens in his drawers, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of them are in drawer $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B.$ Among the pens in drawer $A,$ $\\frac{1}{3}$ of them are blue and, of the pens in drawer $B,$ $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen pen is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the pen was selected from drawer $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the pen was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the pens are in drawer $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{5},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue and $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pens in drawer $B$ are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(U|{A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{4}{5})+(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}+\\frac{1}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+1}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd8f22e9333d6669fcc7a6f46cf10d8d22a1463c2e65ac245db184e6b01e1bab"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A student sits a multiple-choice test. Each question on the test has $n$ possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer to $70%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that they do not know, and the probability that a randomly selected question from the test is answered correctly is $0.76,$ what is the value of $n?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knows the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student doesn't know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $70%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.7,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has $n$ possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected question is answered correctly is $0.76,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.76.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\ 0.76 & =(1)(0.70)+(\\frac{1}{n})(0.3) \\\\ 0.76 & =0.70+\\frac{0.3}{n} \\\\ \\frac{0.3}{n} & =0.06 \\\\ n & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test has $5$ possible answers for each question." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd8f22e9333d6669fcc7a6f46cf10d8d22a1463c2e65ac245db184e6b01e1bab"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-89043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "There are two bags in a box, $A$ and $B.$ In bag $A,$ there are $7$ red marbles and $3$ green marbles. We choose a bag at random with a probability of $\\frac{1}{2}$ each, and then we pick a marble from the chosen bag. The probability that the result of this experiment is a green marble is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected marble from bag $B$ is green?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that bag $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that bag $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ bags, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in bag $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected marble in both bags is green is $\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+P(G|{A}^{′})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 20\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) & =20\\cdot (\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{′}))​ \\\\ 10 & =3+10P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 7 & =10P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ P(G|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected marble from bag $B$ is green is $\\frac{7}{10}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd8f22e9333d6669fcc7a6f46cf10d8d22a1463c2e65ac245db184e6b01e1bab"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"} {"question" "q-122398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973028/state.json" "file_sha256" "56fd160e2245c0ae4723ce191d23e5cd6d7f63a366fc4b33e5b77b0f5f34aaa8"}] "usage" {"q-88581" []} "basis" 758